1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,788
Sam pulled up the assembled "hour eleven" cut on his phone, thumbed the volume to

2
00:00:04,788 --> 00:00:08,239
zero, and scrolled past it the way a stranger on a break would.

3
00:00:08,239 --> 00:00:12,622
Assembly, cutting the avatar clip, the product footage and the captions into one

4
00:00:12,622 --> 00:00:17,169
finished ad, is what this lecture is about, and Sam's test is the one it all has to

5
00:00:17,169 --> 00:00:19,525
pass: does the ad still work with no sound?

6
00:00:19,525 --> 00:00:23,360
When did you last watch a short-form ad on mute, on purpose, to check?

7
00:00:23,360 --> 00:00:27,578
Most people making these ads never have, which is exactly why so many of them

8
00:00:27,578 --> 00:00:31,797
quietly fail the one condition most of their real audience is watching under.

9
00:00:31,797 --> 00:00:36,344
Short-form video defaults to silent, autoplaying, on a phone in someone's hand in a

10
00:00:36,344 --> 00:00:37,056
public place.

11
00:00:37,056 --> 00:00:41,604
If your ad only works with audio, you've lost most of your audience before the hook

12
00:00:41,604 --> 00:00:42,206
even lands.

13
00:00:42,206 --> 00:00:46,370
Design for silence first, and sound becomes something extra your ad has, not

14
00:00:46,370 --> 00:00:47,685
something it depends on.

15
00:00:47,685 --> 00:00:50,150
This isn't a platform quirk you can wait out.

16
00:00:50,150 --> 00:00:54,478
It's a fact about where your ad gets watched: on a bus, in a meeting, next to a

17
00:00:54,478 --> 00:00:55,245
sleeping baby.

18
00:00:55,245 --> 00:00:59,299
Nobody's turning the sound on to give your fifteen seconds a fair hearing.

19
00:00:59,299 --> 00:01:03,901
They decide in the first second whether it's worth the tap, and that decision has to

20
00:01:03,901 --> 00:01:05,490
survive on the picture alone.

21
00:01:06,040 --> 00:01:10,535
Here's the assembly, and notice the captions run under every second of it, not just

22
00:01:10,535 --> 00:01:12,647
the parts where the avatar's on screen.

23
00:01:12,647 --> 00:01:13,621
That's deliberate.

24
00:01:13,621 --> 00:01:18,116
During the product clip, there's no mouth for your viewer to read lips from, so the

25
00:01:18,116 --> 00:01:22,231
caption is the only thing carrying the proof line at all if the sound's off.

26
00:01:22,231 --> 00:01:26,239
And you write the caption text exact, word for word from the script, not a

27
00:01:26,239 --> 00:01:30,733
paraphrase, for the same reason Section 3.4 wouldn't let you let a spec claim drift

28
00:01:30,733 --> 00:01:31,816
in an image overlay.

29
00:01:31,816 --> 00:01:34,795
A caption is still a claim, just delivered differently.

30
00:01:34,795 --> 00:01:39,343
Notice the third line too: "Harlan · link in bio" stays on screen as on-screen text,

31
00:01:39,343 --> 00:01:41,455
separate from the spoken caption track.

32
00:01:41,455 --> 00:01:42,647
That's not decoration.

33
00:01:42,647 --> 00:01:47,195
It's the one instruction a muted viewer actually needs to act, and it has to survive

34
00:01:47,195 --> 00:01:49,145
being read at a glance, not studied.

35
00:01:49,145 --> 00:01:51,257
Timing matters here as much as wording.

36
00:01:51,257 --> 00:01:55,806
A caption that appears half a second after the word is spoken reads as sloppy, and a

37
00:01:55,806 --> 00:02:00,138
caption that outruns the audio reads as a mismatch even when nobody can hear the

38
00:02:00,138 --> 00:02:00,354
gap.

39
00:02:00,354 --> 00:02:04,795
Cut your captions to the same timing marks you built the script against in Section

40
00:02:04,795 --> 00:02:08,044
4.2, not to a rough guess at where a sentence probably ends.

41
00:02:08,594 --> 00:02:12,941
There's a real difference between adding captions to a sound-dependent ad and

42
00:02:12,941 --> 00:02:17,232
actually designing for silence, and it shows up fastest in the first second.

43
00:02:17,232 --> 00:02:21,749
A silence-proof hook puts its key words on your screen immediately, not trailing

44
00:02:21,749 --> 00:02:23,838
half a second behind the spoken line.

45
00:02:23,838 --> 00:02:28,355
A silence-proof CTA holds its text on screen long enough for someone to actually

46
00:02:28,355 --> 00:02:31,855
read it, not just long enough to flash by under a voice track.

47
00:02:31,855 --> 00:02:36,485
Here's the test you can run on your own cut, every time: watch the whole ad muted.

48
00:02:36,485 --> 00:02:41,115
If it still makes sense, you've actually designed it for how most people are going

49
00:02:41,115 --> 00:02:41,680
to see it.

50
00:02:41,680 --> 00:02:46,027
If you find yourself narrating along in your head to fill a gap, you haven't.

51
00:02:46,027 --> 00:02:48,850
Run that test on Harlan's own cut and it holds up.

52
00:02:48,850 --> 00:02:53,593
"Hour eleven" appears as caption text at the exact second it's spoken, and "Harlan ·

53
00:02:53,593 --> 00:02:58,222
link in bio" sits on screen for the full four seconds of the CTA, not just a flash

54
00:02:58,222 --> 00:02:58,843
at the end.

55
00:02:59,393 --> 00:03:01,807
One honest check before this ships anywhere.

56
00:03:01,807 --> 00:03:06,249
It's easy to treat captions as an afterthought, auto-generated and barely glanced

57
00:03:06,249 --> 00:03:09,924
at, because they feel like a formatting detail rather than content.

58
00:03:09,924 --> 00:03:10,582
They aren't.

59
00:03:10,582 --> 00:03:14,970
If you let a caption misquote the mmHg spec, that's exactly the same risk as the

60
00:03:14,970 --> 00:03:19,358
garbled overlay from Section 3.2, just delivered through a different part of the

61
00:03:19,358 --> 00:03:19,687
frame.

62
00:03:19,687 --> 00:03:23,855
Give it the same care you'd give any other line a customer might screenshot.

63
00:03:23,855 --> 00:03:27,475
Auto-generated captions make this easier to get wrong, not harder.

64
00:03:27,475 --> 00:03:31,972
A speech-to-text pass can mishear a number, drop a unit, or smooth an exact phrase

65
00:03:31,972 --> 00:03:35,263
into something that sounds similar and isn't the same claim.

66
00:03:35,263 --> 00:03:39,761
Read every generated caption against the script it came from, line by line, before

67
00:03:39,761 --> 00:03:40,474
you trust it.

68
00:03:40,474 --> 00:03:45,081
That's a two-minute check against a mistake that, once it's running as a paid ad, is

69
00:03:45,081 --> 00:03:47,110
a great deal more expensive to catch.

70
00:03:47,660 --> 00:03:51,936
One thing worth holding onto before you leave this lecture: there's a real gap

71
00:03:51,936 --> 00:03:56,431
between an ad that technically has captions and one that was actually designed for

72
00:03:56,431 --> 00:03:56,870
silence.

73
00:03:56,870 --> 00:04:00,433
Plenty of ads that fail the muted-watch test still have captions.

74
00:04:00,433 --> 00:04:04,928
They just got bolted on after a cut built for sound, instead of built into the cut

75
00:04:04,928 --> 00:04:05,750
from the start.

76
00:04:05,750 --> 00:04:09,971
Design silence-first, and captioning stops being an extra pass at the end and

77
00:04:09,971 --> 00:04:13,151
becomes part of the same decision as the shot list itself.

78
00:04:13,151 --> 00:04:17,646
Next lecture is where all of this becomes three real, finished ads for B04 instead

79
00:04:17,646 --> 00:04:19,181
of a set of separate pieces.

80
00:04:19,181 --> 00:04:23,731
Before then, do Sam's test on your own Section 4.3 clip: watch it back muted, right

81
00:04:23,731 --> 00:04:23,950
now.

82
00:04:23,950 --> 00:04:27,130
If it still lands with no sound, you're ready to assemble.

83
00:04:27,130 --> 00:04:31,515
If it doesn't, fix the caption timing tonight, not partway through the workshop.
